<!-- first drafts of this preview were written by both Steven Clontz and Chrissy Safranski after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->
<!-- most of what's here is Chrissy's version -->

<setup>
  <math name="E">2.718281828</math>
</setup>
<p>
    Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m> near <m>a = 0</m>.
    Let's approximate the value of <m>f(x)</m> using the linear approximation at <m>a=0</m>,
    <me>
      L(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-0)
    </me>
  </p>
<!-- 
    <figure xml:id="F-8a-1-Intro">
      <caption>The function <m>f(x)=e^x</m> and its tangent line <m>L(x)=1+x</m> near the point <m>(0,f(0))</m>.</caption>
      <image width="75%" source="images/8a_1_intro2" />
    </figure> -->

  <p>
    <ol marker="a.">
      <li>
        <p>
          Find <m>f(0)</m>, <m>f'(x)</m>, <m>f'(0)</m>, and <m>L(x)</m>.</p>
        <p>
          <m>f(0)=</m><answer name="f0">1</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>f'(x)=</m><answer>e^x</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>f'(0)=</m><answer name="fp0">1</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>L(x)=f(0)+f'(0)(x-0)=</m><mathInput disabled>$f0</mathInput><m>+</m><mathInput disabled>$fp0</mathInput><m>(x-0)=</m><answer name="LxExp">1+x</answer>.<setup><function name="Lx">$LxExp</function></setup>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          A graph of <m>f(x)=e^x</m> and <m>L(x)=</m><mathInput disabled>$Lx</mathInput> is shown below.  Complete the sentence that follows to describe how <m>L(x)</m> approximates <m>f(x)</m> near <m>a = 0</m>.
        </p>
        <graph xmin="-2" ymin="-1" xmax="2" ymax="3" showNavigation="false" fixAxes="true">
          <function>e^x<label><m>f(x)</m></label></function>
          <function styleNumber="2">$LxExp<label><m>L(x)</m></label></function>
        </graph>
        <p>
          At <m>x = 0</m>, the linear approximation <m>L(x)</m> has the exact same <choiceInput name="a1" inline>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>value</choice>
          <choice>value and slope</choice>
          <choice>value, slope, and concavity</choice>
        </choiceInput> as <m>f(x)</m>, so near <m>x = 0</m>, the approximation is very good, but as we move further away from <m>x = 0</m>, the concavity of the function <m>f(x)</m> means that the value of <m>f(x)</m> <choiceInput inline name="a2">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>gets further from</choice>
          <choice>gets closer to</choice>
          <choice>maintains the same difference from</choice>
        </choiceInput> the value of <m>L(x)</m>.
      </p>   
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>$a1.selectedIndices=3 and $a2.selectedIndices=2</when></award></answer>
      </p>
      </li>   
      <li>
        <p>
           Build a spreadsheet that computes the difference between <m>f(x)</m> and <m>L(x)</m> for <m>x</m>-values between <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>, spaced <m>0.1</m> units apart.  Record your results to at least 5 decimal places of accuracy. <alert>Note:</alert> we will revisit this spreadsheet for additional computations in later activities
<!--           <xref ref="act-8a-1-3">Activity</xref>,  -->
          so it would be ideal if you save your work for later reference.
        </p>          
        <p>
           Your spreadsheet should start like this:
              <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
                <row left="minor">
                  <cell right="minor">Row Number</cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>\Delta x</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>x</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>f(x) = e^x</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>L(x) = 1+x</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row left="minor">
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.1</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>-1.0</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.36788</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row left="minor">
                  <cell right="minor"><m>1</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.1</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>-0.9</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.40657</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row left="minor">
                  <cell right="minor"><m>2</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.1</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>-0.8</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.44933</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>0.2</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
<!--             </table> -->
        </p>
        <p>
          Check your work by entering your results from Row 14 in the provided blanks below. 
          <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
                <row left="minor">
                  <cell right="minor">Row Number</cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>\Delta x</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>x</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>f(x) = e^x</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><m>L(x) = 1+x</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row left="minor">
                  <cell right="minor"><m>14</m></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><answer>0.1</answer></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><answer>0.4</answer></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001">e^0.4</answer></cell>
                  <cell right="minor"><answer>1.4</answer></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Next, add a column to your spreadsheet that computes the absolute error, <m>|f(x)-L(x)|</m>. 
        </p> 
        <p>
          The new entry in the absolute error column of Row 14 is <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001">e^.4-1.4</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          How do the absolute differences between <m>f(x)</m> and <m>L(x)</m> change as we increase the value of <m>x</m> across the interval from <m>x = -1</m> to <m>x = 1</m>?
          <answer inline>
            <choice>?</choice>
            <choice>The differences don't change</choice>
            <choice>The differences get smaller and smaller</choice>
            <choice>The differences get larger and larger</choice>
            <choice credit="1">The differences are larger the farther away <m>x</m> is from <m>0</m></choice>
  </answer>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          For about what values of <m>x</m> is it true that <m>|f(x)-L(x)| \lt 0.1</m>? For example, if your spreadsheet shows that the first times the error is greater than or equal to <m>0.1</m> are <m>-0.7</m> and <m>0.8</m>, then enter the interval <m>(-0.7,0.8)</m>. 
          <answer matchPartial>(-0.5,0.4)</answer>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Notice that the curvature in <m>f(x) = e^x</m> is what makes the linear approximation <m>L(x)</m> lose accuracy.  What kind of simple function might we expect to do a better job approximating <m>e^x</m> than a linear one?
          <answer inline>
            <choice>?</choice>
            <choice>constant</choice>
            <choice credit="1">quadratic</choice>
            <choice>trigonometric</choice>
            <choice>rational</choice>
  </answer>
        </p>
      </li>   
    </ol>
  </p>